    <string name="tools_plan_assumed_rates">Descent %1$s %2$s (%3$s), ascent %4$s %5$s (%6$s).</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_assumed_model">Model: a 3 min stop at 5 m; rock bottom at twice the combined gas use for 1 minute, an ascent no faster than 9 m/min and 10 bar; air and nitrox 21 to 40 % to 40 m; real gas in the cylinder.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_halves">Halves</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_thirds">Thirds</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_sixths">Sixths</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_rock_bottom_about">Rock bottom: you go up when the gas left is what two divers need to reach the surface from that depth, both breathing at twice their normal rate for one minute, then a direct ascent at 9 m/min, plus 10 bar. Never below 50 bar here. Taught by UTD and GUE; Subsurface and GasPlanner calculate it too.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_rock_bottom_limits">It needs your real SAC. It assumes the problem happens at that depth and says nothing about a long swim back.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_reserve_about">Fixed reserve: you finish the dive with this much left in the cylinder, so the ascent and the safety stop are counted on top. Dive centres and agencies teach a fixed reserve, from about 30 to 50 bar.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_reserve_limits">A fixed number ignores depth: at 30 m it is well below rock bottom, at 12 m it is generous.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_ascent_about">Fixed ascent pressure: you start going up when the gauge reaches this pressure. “Up at 100 bar” is a common dive-centre rule.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_ascent_limits">A fixed number ignores depth and group size; compare it with rock bottom, which is always shown.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_halves_about">Halves: half the gas for the way out, half for the way back. Taught for dives that return the way they went. In a group the smaller supply decides how many litres are spent before turning.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_halves_limits">It leaves nothing in reserve: not enough for trouble on an open-water ascent.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_thirds_about">Thirds: a third out, a third back, a third in reserve. Taught for cave and wreck diving. In a group the smaller supply decides how many litres are spent before turning.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_sixths_about">Sixths: a sixth out, a sixth back, the rest kept. Used for demanding overhead dives where the way back is long or slow. In a group the smaller supply decides how many litres are spent before turning.</string>
    <string name="tools_plan_rule_overhead_limits">For overhead diving with training; it is not a rule for open water.</string>
</resources>
